WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.480
We all want to eat
the kinds of foods

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.400
that make us feel
better and live longer.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.520
But there’s so much conflicting
information out there,

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.980
so many nutrition opinions.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.160
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.020
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.660
It’s my job to give you
the information you need

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.120
to make the healthiest
choices possible.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:27.060
Did you know that those who eat spicy
foods regularly tend to live longer?

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.648
Here’s our first story.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.540
Most people could name
four of the basic tastes:

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.880
sweet, sour, salty, and bitter.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.060
The rare foodie may
be able to nail umami,

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.800
but what about “the forgotten
ﬂavour sense,” spicy?

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.820
This seeming neglect is somewhat
surprising given that hot peppers

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.580
are one of the world's
most widely-used spices.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:55.020
In fact, as many as one
in four people on the planet

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.920
currently eat chilies on a daily
basis, raising the question,

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.160
“Why is spicy
food so popular?”

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:08.220
Why do some like it hot, enjoying
what is, at ﬁrst glance,

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.100
an irritating, and potentially
painful mouth sensation?

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.500
According to one
popular suggestion,

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.360
it is the endorphin hit,

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.120
the release of your natural
morphine-like

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.920
painkiller chemicals
from your brain.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.700
You hear this a lot in the popular
press, but we still don’t have

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.040
any convincing evidence
to support it.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.980
Another suggestion is that
we learned to use it

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.820
for its antimicrobial properties
before the age of refrigeration,

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.120
given that spicy food tends
to be preferred by cultures

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.240
living in warmer climates,
and for that matter,

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.340
maybe it makes us sweat and thereby
ultimately helps cool us down.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:49.080
Then again, maybe it’s just
because people just like the taste.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:53.400
Preference for spicy foods does
seem to run in families.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.400
Based on twin studies comparing
the preferences of identical

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.400
to fraternal twins, genetic
factors may account

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.520
for up to half of the variation,
similar to the heritability

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.560
of sweet and sour preferences.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.420
It may also be hormonal.
Researchers in France noted

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.580
that particularly males
liked spicy food

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.820
and wondered if it was
a testosterone thing.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.460
So, they took a group
of more than 100 men

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.140
and gave them a plate
of mashed potatoes

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.500
and asked them to Tabasco
the potatoes to taste,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.420
after taking saliva samples
to measure their testosterone levels.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.020
And what do you know, men
who had higher testosterone

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.580
added more hot sauce.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.280
That may help explain
this study that found

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.100
a correlation between
hot chili pepper consumption

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.220
and muscle strength
in adult males.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.880
As an aside, when I was looking
at the testosterone literature,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.540
I ran across this study
that showed young men experience

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.660
an acute decrease in blood
testosterone levels

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:56.220
after chugging sugar water
or whey protein powder:

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.900
12- to 18-year-olds were randomized
to a few scoops of protein power

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.920
or about two cans of soda worth
of sugar, and within 20 minutes

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.620
saw a significant drop
in testosterone levels

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.920
compared to a sugar-free,
protein-free control.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.760
This is consistent with lower
testosterone levels

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.880
found in people on high
protein, low carb diets.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.360
Anyway, what does
the consumption

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.900
of hot spicy foods
do to our lifespan?

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.680
Well, a massive study of a half
million men and women in China

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.560
found that those regularly
eating spicy foods

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.820
had an associated 14 percent
reduction in total mortality,

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.100
meaning the risk
of premature death.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:43.380
That could translate into about
an extra year onto your lifespan,

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.140
if it is cause and effect.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.440
Those of you who
follow my work know

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.320
there are two main potential
issues with observational studies:

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.680
reverse causality
and confounding factors.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.200
In other words,
instead of spicy foods

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.320
leading to less disease,
maybe disease is leading

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.400
to less spicy food, with sick
people eating blander diets.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.100
However, the apparent
benefit remained

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.020
even after excluding
sick folks,

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.760
or those who were just
about to croak.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.240
And so, reverse causality
doesn’t seem to explain it,

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:19.200
though hey, people who eat
spicy food probably drink more,

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.880
and what do they
drink in China?

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.900
Green tea; so, maybe
that’s what going on?

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.820
It would be nice
to replicate this

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:31.020
in a non-green tea drinking
country or even a country

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:34.440
that drinks terrible stuff,
like the United States.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:39.000
And yet same thing, a 13 percent
risk reduction in premature death.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.940
And the protective
association remained

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:45.120
even after controlling for
Mexican-American ethnicity.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.260
As I detailed before, they have
a longevity advantage,

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.140
presumably because
they eat so many beans.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.560
Same thing was found
in Italy, this time

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.440
a 23 percent lower
all-cause mortality.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.860
That’d be like two
extra years of life.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.400
The authors conclude
that minor dietary changes,

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.100
such as just adding chilies
to one’s usual diet,

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:11.700
could be a valuable measure
for improving health.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.760
There have been four
studies done overall,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.120
including this one in Iran,
that all found the same thing:

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.780
significantly lower risk
of premature death.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:24.900
In fact, black or chili pepper
consumption was associated

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.080
with about the same
lifespan extension as turmeric,

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.360
even at just like
a pinch a day.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:33.540
And, those that did both
seemed to do even better,

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.420
which is consistent with the ability
of a black pepper compound

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:40.560
to boost the bioavailability
of the turmeric compound curcumin.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.460
I’ve got about 50 videos on why
turmeric is so good for you,

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.280
but why might chili peppers
extend your life?

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.861
We’ll find out, next.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.460
Four out of four studies
on spicy food and mortality

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:00.420
found a significant decrease
in the risk of premature death.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.080
The intake of sweet peppers
also seemed to help,

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.320
but to a lesser extent;
so, there may be some benefit

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.533
to the spicy capsaicin
compound itself.

00:06:10.533 --> 00:06:10.597
I have a whole section on chili
peppers in my Fat Burners chapter

00:06:10.597 --> 00:06:12.780
in How Not to Diet:
how cayenne pepper can counteract

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.360
the metabolic slowing
that accompanies weight loss

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.820
and accelerate
fat burning as a bonus.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:24.060
So, maybe the weight loss benefits
account for the mortality benefits?

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:27.780
Apparently not, since there was
a mortality risk reduction

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:29.220
with chili pepper
consumption

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:32.520
even after controlling
for body mass index.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:36.120
Maybe the spice was used
as a replacement for salt?

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:39.840
Anything that reduces sodium
intake could improve longevity,

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.280
as excess salt consumption
is the deadliest dietary risk factor,

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.280
the worst thing
about the human diet,

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.060
wiping out millions
of people every year.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.600
Not only could you use
the spice to replace salt,

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:57.420
the spicy compound
actually makes things

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.940
taste saltier than
they actually are.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.580
Sprinkling on some
red pepper powder

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:05.400
can increase your salt
taste sensitivity;

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.240
so, you can achieve the same
salty taste with less salt.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:12.840
You can put people in a PET
scanner and pick up differences

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:16.440
in their secondary taste cortex,
the part of your brain

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.320
associated with pleasure
signaling to salty foods.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:23.940
So, you can use hot peppers
to hack your brain for your health.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.880
And indeed, those with
a high spice preference

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:29.760
had lower salt intake
and better blood pressures,

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.980
and this again appears
to be independent

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:35.340
of the anti-obesity benefit
of hot pepper consumption,

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.580
though the lower risk
of developing high blood pressure

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.220
among those with higher
hot pepper intake

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:44.340
was also independent
of sodium intake;

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:47.520
so, there may be some other
benefit pathways as well

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:51.780
like maybe it also helps your
kidneys excrete more salt too,

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:54.960
though the evidence
to support this was derived

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.540
from studies on mice;
so, who knows.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.380
However it works, eating
spicy foods or adding

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.080
supplemental spicy flavor
to food by like

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.660
sprinkling on cayenne
pepper or hot sauce

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:09.480
represents a novel
lifestyle intervention

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:12.240
that can reduce both salt
intake and blood pressure.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:12.780
Here are some examples
of no-salt added hot sauces.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:17.160
Even regular tabasco
is pretty low in sodium,

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:19.800
though only
the original flavor.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:21.900
Some of their other
spin-off flavors like

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:25.200
buffalo/habanero/chipotle
have 5 times more.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.700
Now, just because all studies
on spicy food and mortality to date

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:32.160
suggest hot peppers
may help you live longer

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:35.880
doesn’t mean you can go out
and eat a ghost pepper,

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:40.283
designated the Guinness book’s
hottest pepper in the world in 2007.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:46.500
Some varieties of habanero can be
50 times as hot as jalapenos.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.680
To get to ghost
peppers, though,

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.620
you have to switch over
to millions of units.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.980
The ghost pepper beat out
the habanero in 2007,

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:01.140
which itself got out shadowed
by the Trinidad Scorpion in 2011,

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:07.560
and then 2013, burning as the
reigning champ, the Carolina Reaper.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:11.640
The only thing hotter than
the reaper is pure pepper spray,

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:16.380
which can lead to such violent
coughing you can rupture a lung.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.460
Pepper spray in the eye
only seems to be a problem

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:22.020
if you can’t wash it out.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.480
The best thing you can do
if you’re trying to help someone

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.540
sprayed in the face
is try to calm them down,

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:31.560
make sure they’re breathing ok,
remove their contact lenses

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:35.220
as soon as possible,
and then, abundantly irrigate

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.380
their eyes to wash
out the chemical.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:43.680
Really abundantly, as in washing
their eyes with water or saline

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:47.880
for a full 10-20 minutes,
which is a long time

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.560
to be washing your eye out.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.980
Again, after contact lenses
have been removed,

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:55.680
or even better not
worn to an event

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.140
where you might be pepper
sprayed in the first place.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:02.160
Even just ambient exposure
to pepper gas can cause

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:04.920
dry eye symptoms
that last for weeks,

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:06.780
even if you’re not
sprayed directly.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:10.800
It’s ironic that the
Chemical Weapons Convention

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:14.460
prohibits the use of these riot
control agents during warfare;

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:20.280
yet, they are routinely used
to quell civilian protest.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:22.740
The most serious eye injuries
though, are trauma

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.500
from fired projectiles
like pepper balls,

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:28.140
leading to the suggestion
that protestors wear

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:31.800
ballistic eye protection,
recommending that medical centers

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.620
proactively reach out to protest
leaders and participants

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:37.260
regarding appropriate
safety precautions.

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:41.040
In terms of what you can
do for pepper spray irritation

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.620
on your skin, there
are anecdotal reports

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.120
that baby shampoo is helpful.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.660
However, as yet, there have been
no published scientific studies

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:53.760
that demonstrate their
effectiveness until...now.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.580
“Baby Shampoo to Relieve
the Discomfort

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:58.320
of Tear Gas and Pepper
Spray Exposure:

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.480
A Randomized Controlled Trial.”

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:04.320
Police recruits received a burst
of pepper spray to the face,

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:06.960
then were randomized
to washing off with water

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:11.520
or water along with some Johnson
&amp; Johnson baby shampoo and…

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:14.640
it didn’t work at all.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.160
How about using Maalox,

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:20.040
or a numbing lidocaine
gel, or milk?

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:24.480
Nothing beat out
plain old water.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:29.160
Copious water decontamination
is the preferred method

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:32.580
of pain control after topical
pepper spray exposure.

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:35.760
Anyways, that was quite
the tangent.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:39.960
Bottomline, should we all
begin taking tablets of capsaicin

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:41.940
and dousing our food
with hot sauce?

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:46.020
If you like hot sauce, go for it,
but I don’t think we should start

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:48.300
taking supplements
until we have randomized,

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:51.326
controlled trials proving benefit.

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:54.360
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:57.060
